/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:97:''THE CUSTODY DOOR OPENS THE VESSEL'S OWN KEY, AND THE CREDENTIAL TAKES ONE ROAD.'' A vessel stands holding no vessel key: it binds its socket, serves the public shelf, reads no private row and mints nothing. `lares vault unlock` reads a credential on a TTY and sends ONE custody frame on the owner-only socket; the frame meets the custody door ahead of every verb, so it never becomes an invocation and leaves no digest, receipt, outcome or log line. The door hands the credential to the one holder in node-main, which opens the key and the custody root; the standing composition then tears down whole and a fresh one stands on the same floor with what the key opens. `lares vault lock` drops the key and returns the vessel to the floor, and so does every restart: nothing an unlock did touches disk. A refusal names its reading (`no-slot-opens` · `tree-absent` · `tree-unreadable` · `attempts-spent`) and never the input; three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts, and only a restart restores them. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:99:''A SEALED ROW'S ACT RIDES A CUSTODY FRAME, NEVER A STORE VERB'' (operator ruling). A store verb's output rests in the daemon's document in the clear, and the digest of its arguments rests in a receipt, so a roster key, a pet-name or a handle-book snapshot on that road would leave its sealed row for an unsealed document. A command that reads or writes a sealed row sends one `act` frame on the owner-only socket instead: the custody door runs the act on the vessel's main thread, against the key a person already opened, and answers that one caller. ''One typed table bounds the acts'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`). A frame names an act of the table and that act's typed arguments, and nothing beside them; an act the table does not name refuses by name, and so does an argument a row does not name. No row takes a credential of the vessel, and none hands out a seed or anything that rebuilds one. The restore of a face's backup bundle rides two rows that take the bundle's body still sealed and the bundle's own key, which opens that one body and nothing the vessel holds (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore">>). A root comes in through that door and none goes out through it: a root's recovery split hands a person two shares that rebuild the root, so it runs in that person's own process alone and refuses beside a standing vessel. No row signs whatever it gets handed: the one row that signs as a persona root reads the image first and signs the seal ceremony's own two images alone, a charter roll and a crossing record. The door logs an act's name and how it ended; nothing an act frame carries or answers reaches a receipt, an outcome or a log. ''One key session opens a vessel's rows.'' Beside a standing vessel a command opens none of its own: an unlocked vessel answers the act with no second prompt, and a locked one refuses and names `lares vault unlock`. With no vessel standing, the command runs the same act of the same table in its own process and opens its own session at the first sealed row the act touches. ''The fleet mirror of a face's own names rides the same door.'' A pet-name and a declared Handle rest sealed on their vessel and ride that face's OWN `@persona` plane to that face's other vessels. ''One face, one plane'': a name of the face at one handle-index lands on the plane of the group that face's sealed anchors name, and on no other, so a vessel that holds several faces tells no face's fleet about another face. After the sealed act lands, the command sends one more act frame that names the face and which of its two names, and no name: `selves.carry` reads the name off its own sealed row and writes it onto the plane from the vessel's main thread, through the Repo that holds the plane's document, and `selves.clear` clears a name once its sealed row holds it no more. Neither act moves a plane past what the sealed row says: a carry refuses where the row holds no such name, a clear refuses while the row still holds it, and both refuse a face this vessel holds no root for, so a vessel that never held a name clears nobody's. ''A carry lands only on a plane whose rest seals'': each plane names where its document rests, and while that rest stands clear on the vessel's disk the carry refuses by it and the name stays on its sealed row, while a clear still runs (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed">>). `lares persona sync` carries with `fill`, which lands a name only where the plane never held one: a device cannot tell its own older name from a newer one another vessel set, so a sync replaces neither a name nor a clear, and a rename on the device replaces it. The write needs no vessel key and crosses no worker as a message; it rides the custody door all the same, since that door carries the one frame that leaves no receipt, and since a locked vessel shuts every fleet lane. `selves.read` answers each carried face as its own plane names it, to its one caller. No verb takes or answers a face's own name, and a mirror act leaves no outcome, receipt, argument digest or log line behind it. The daemon island mounts the worn face's plane, so that plane's own document syncs between the main thread's Repo and the island's, as the plane itself; nothing beside the plane's document carries a name across that port. A command with no vessel standing reaches no plane: the act refuses by name before it opens any row, and the name stays on its sealed row until `lares persona sync` carries it. A clear made while no plane answered stays on its own vessel, since a sync carries names and never an absence: the person clears the name again beside a standing vessel, and that clear reaches the plane. ''How far a verb's record travels'': the daemon's own document rests in the main thread's Repo and in the vessel's own daemon island. Among socket peers it leaves a vessel toward one class alone, a socket the gate sorts same-operator (a holder of admin on the daemon bag, or a device the pinned persona KEL delegated), which the share verdict full-syncs (`packages/lararium-node/src/self-slot-share.ts`). A sibling, a contracted carrier, a walker and a stranger never read it. So anything a store verb records reaches each of the operator's own delegated devices that dials the vessel, and no one beyond them.
