/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:286:- The **inception event** creates **two** key sets: the *initial/current* set (public keys included in the event) and the *next* set, committed only as a **cryptographic digest** — so the next keys are **hidden** until used.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:291:- **Recovery via superseding rotation:** "a rotation event may be used to supersede an interaction event. When this happens the key event log **forks** at the superseding rotation event." For delegated identifiers this "enable[s] recovery even in the event of the **joint compromise** of a delegating identifier's signing keys and the delegated identifier's pre-rotated keys," and nests to multiple delegation levels.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:293:**Why this matters for you:** pre-rotation is the one design in this survey that **eliminates the dual-authority window by construction**. There is no period where two authorities are both live, because the successor authority is *committed but hidden* in advance and the KEL's total ordering makes exactly one successor valid. An attacker who compromises the current keys still cannot rotate, because they do not know the pre-committed next keys — only their digest.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:359:1. **Prefer pre-rotation (KERI-style) over an overlap window if you can afford it.** It is the only surveyed model where the dual-authority hazard does not exist, and it makes single-sig → multi-sig a *rotation*, not a *migration*. **Confidence: 15/20.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/topic-a-the-onboarding-cost-question-1c7848.mem:383:| KERI pre-rotation eliminates the overlap window | 12/20 |
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:104:Pre-rotation commits **digests of a list of next public keys plus a next threshold** `nt`. A rotation event *"MUST include a list of the set of pre-rotated public keys (not their digests) thereby exposing them and MUST be signed by a set of private keys from these newly exposed… keypairs that satisfy the pre-rotated threshold."* There is no secret reconstruction anywhere in the protocol. **Fork B is not an alternative to KERI's model; it is KERI's model.**
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:122:CESR **controller-indexed signatures** bind each signature to a *position* in the ordered key list. A rotation event's signatures carry **two indices** — one into the new current signing key list, one into the *prior next key digest list*:
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:124:> *"the second index is used to look up the public key digest from the prior next key digest list, then the first index is used to look up the exposed public key from the signing key list, then the digest is verified against the exposed public key, and finally, the doubly indexed signature is verified using the exposed public key."*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:140:Consequence: a compromise of a *signing* key is recoverable by rotation (A0). A compromise of the *pre-rotated rotation* keys is **not recoverable at all for a non-delegated AID**. And even for a delegated AID, *"recovery can not happen for any compromise of pre-rotated keys, only the **latest-seen**… Recovery is possible after the first compromised rotation by superseding it but **not after the subsequent** compromised rotation."*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:241:### The structural answer: pre-rotation makes the dual-authority window impossible
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:243:Each KERI establishment event commits to **exactly one** next key set and **one** next threshold. A rotation must satisfy **both** the new current threshold **and** the prior-next threshold. So an A→B migration is **one rotation event**, signed by the reconstructed Fork-A recovery root (satisfying prior-next) *and* by the incoming guardian keys (satisfying new-current), committing the guardians' public-key digests as the new pre-rotated set. **One transient reconstruction, ever. No period in which both authorities are independently sufficient.** This is TUF's "signed by both old and new" rule expressed in KERI's grammar.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:332:**Guardian churn is cheaper under Fork B, not more expensive.** Replacing one guardian in Fork B is *one rotation event* committing a new pre-rotated set — the same operation as recovery, already implemented. Replacing one card in Fork A requires re-splitting the recovery root, which requires reconstructing it. Unless you add **proactive secret sharing** (Herzberg/Jarecki/Krawczyk/Yung; Ostrovsky–Yung mobile-adversary model), which lets the honest holders regenerate a lost share *and* periodically re-randomize all shares so an attacker must collect k within one epoch. That is a genuine Fork-A hardening you should price before ruling — but it adds a periodic multi-party protocol, which is most of the complexity Fork A was chosen to avoid.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:334:**Multi-persona compartments break the symmetry.** Fork B lets one guardian keypair be committed into some personas' pre-rotation lists and deliberately not others, at zero extra custody cost — disjoint guardian sets per compartment. Fork A costs a fresh card per persona per guardian. ICN names the correlated hazard on the other side: *"A person recovering after device loss rotates in **every context within one window**, and an observer of two coops' logs correlates on timing. R6.4 is weakened by the very auditability R9.2 requires — a genuine tension between two requirements, not an oversight."* And on shared guardians: *"Shared guardians are better UX and worse privacy (the guardian learns the linkage); per-context is the reverse."*
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/swarm-harvest/key-recovery-custody-fork-outside-research-r-620622.mem:375:| KERI pre-rotation is Fork B; no reconstruction anywhere in the protocol | 19/20 |
